<p>Afghanistan’s Taliban authorities said a Pakistani airstrike hit the Omid drug rehabilitation hospital in Kabul on Monday night, killing at least 400 people and injuring about 250, in what several outlets described as a sharp escalation in the neighbors’ cross-border conflict. Pakistan rejected that account and said its forces instead carried out precision strikes on military installations and what it called terrorist support infrastructure in Kabul and Nangarhar as part of Operation Ghazab lil-Haq. Pakistani officials linked the operation to security threats and said it followed unprovoked firing from across the border after weeks of hostilities that began in late February. Afghan officials denied supporting attacks inside Pakistan and accused Islamabad of hitting a medical facility, with patients among the dead and wounded. The competing claims have raised fears of further retaliation and deepened concern about civilian protection as international calls for a ceasefire continue.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/9kEQHexU5xCAHEJbQwWNB28sVXFx6b28f5VnY5evmr896J0VhdHu4DEwNC3FcpzjpQpvtPVfjUeLSpIFS_GlAhUbdnSCzInQxBNUL1v6zqMVg6Rnm_sEvzFrgQGZM_lvCe1oWstwzGCLyJyTjKqnp0sCmoD_blbNogDh1EqXKRNZM_XRD88PhdPsnmAreQqWYT5fqSTF5x4Ybq9z0L-FQvb3RP35NQ' alt='Scene in Kabul after the reported strike as tensions between Pakistan and Afghanistan escalated.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Survivor account: A survivor identified as Ahmad told Reuters-partner coverage that flames engulfed people inside the rehabilitation center after the strike, and described the scene as &quot;like doomsday&quot;.</li><li>Information battle: Pakistan said strike footage showed secondary detonations consistent with ammunition depots, while Afghan media circulated video of a building engulfed in flames, underscoring how both sides used imagery to back incompatible accounts of the target.</li><li>Domestic reaction: Afghan cricketers, including Rashid Khan, publicly condemned the strike, saying attacks on medical infrastructure, whether intentional or accidental, amount to a war crime.</li><li>Regional mediation: Coverage cited China’s Foreign Ministry as directly mediating between Pakistan and Afghanistan as the confrontation widened.</li></ul><blockquote>All targeting has been done with precision, only at those infrastructures being used by the Afghan Taliban regime to support its multiple terror proxies, including Fitna Al Khawarij and Fitna Al Hindustan, as can be clearly seen in the accompanying footage. - Attaullah Tarar</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Taliban government: Suhail Shaheen told NDTV that Afghanistan would &quot;respond in their language,&quot; signaling that Kabul saw the strike as something it would not answer only through diplomatic protest. (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/pakistan-afghanistan-war-kabul-hospital-taliban-official-suhail-shaheen-to-ndtv-will-respond-in-their-language-11226071#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li><li>Pakistan government: Information Minister Attaullah Tarar said the targeted infrastructure was being used to support what Islamabad calls Fitna Al Khawarij and Fitna Al Hindustan, framing the raid as counterterrorism rather than an attack on civilians. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1982625/pakistani-forces-strike-afghan-talibans-military-installations-in-kabul-nangarhar-info-minister'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Kabul residents: Locals reported fighter jets flying low over western Kabul and anti-aircraft fire after multiple explosions, offering a citywide view of the night beyond the official dispute over the target. (<a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/blasts-hit-kabul-as-taliban-claims-of-pakistani-airstrikes-locals-report-jets-overhead-13990183.html'>Firstpost</a>, <a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/pakistan-afghanistan-war-live-updates-kabul-hospital-airstrike-taliban-reactions-military-attacks-liveblog-13990224.html'>Firstpost</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https:/